# Buddy — narrow NOPASSWD rules so setup_ros2_jazzy.sh can run unattended over SSH.
#
# INSTALL (on the Jetson, NOT on the Mac):
#   sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/buddy-setup
# then paste this file's contents (minus this header if you like) and save.
#
# Use visudo, never a plain editor or `cp`. visudo validates syntax on save; a
# malformed file in /etc/sudoers.d/ can break sudo for every user on the box and
# the recovery path is a physical console. Verify afterwards:
#   sudo visudo -c
#
# Username and binary paths below were VERIFIED on the actual Jetson 2026-07-31
# (user `venti`; note udevadm is /usr/bin/udevadm on this JetPack image, not
# /usr/sbin as on some Ubuntu builds — a wrong path here does not error, it just
# silently fails to match and sudo falls back to prompting, which hangs an
# unattended run). Re-verify with `command -v` if the image is ever reflashed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HONEST SCOPE NOTE — read before installing.
#
# These rules are narrower than blanket NOPASSWD, but they are NOT a security
# boundary. `apt install` as root can install any package, which is arbitrary
# code execution as root by definition. The real reduction here is in blast
# radius from accidents and from casual misuse, not from a determined attacker
# who already has the `buddy` user account.
#
# What this genuinely buys:
#   - No blanket `sudo <anything>` if the account is compromised.
#   - `cp` is pinned to one destination directory instead of arbitrary
#     root-owned file writes (which would otherwise be a trivial root escalation).
#
# If you want the exposure gone after bring-up: delete this file once
# setup_ros2_jazzy.sh has run successfully. Nothing in the day-to-day robot
# workflow (colcon build, ros2 launch, running tests) needs sudo at all.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Package management — needed by steps 1 and 2 of setup_ros2_jazzy.sh
venti ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/apt, /usr/bin/apt-get, /usr/bin/dpkg
venti ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/add-apt-repository

# Locale generation — step 1
venti ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/locale-gen

# rosdep initialisation — step 2 (`sudo rosdep init`)
venti ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/rosdep

# udev rule install — step 4. `cp` is pinned to the rules directory on purpose:
# an unpinned `sudo cp` is equivalent to root file-write anywhere, which would
# make every other restriction in this file pointless.
venti ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/cp * /etc/udev/rules.d/
venti ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/udevadm
